=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on mswitch0, the Campus 1 multilayer switch, not on router11 as
the on-call engineer suspects.

mswitch0 serves as the L3 gateway for all Campus 1 PCs (PC5–PC14) across
VLANs 10, 20, and 30, with directly configured interfaces at 172.16.10.254,
172.16.20.254, and 172.16.30.254. It is supposed to run ospfd (the FRR daemons
file has `ospfd=yes`) to participate in the OSPF domain via its uplink to
router16 (10.2.2.0/24), learning backbone routes and advertising its VLAN
prefixes to the rest of the network.

The root cause is that /etc/frr/ospfd.conf on mswitch0 was emptied (wiped to
zero bytes). As a result, ospfd failed to start with any usable configuration,
and the FRR service entered a `failed` state. Only zebra, mgmtd, and staticd
remain running — ospfd is absent. Without ospfd, mswitch0's kernel routing
table contains only its four directly-connected prefixes; it has no route to
the Campus 2 subnets (10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24) or any other backbone
network. When PC5 pings 10.5.0.1, mswitch0 itself sends back ICMP "Destination
Net Unreachable" (confirmed live), proving the drop occurs at the Campus 1
gateway, not at router11.

The stale LSA from mswitch0 (router-id 172.16.30.254, age ~388 s at time of
capture) is still present in the OSPF database of all routers, which is why
router11 and others continue to show 172.16.x.x routes — these will age out
(MaxAge = 3600 s) as no router is refreshing them. Router11 is fully healthy
and restarting it will not restore connectivity.

Corrective fix: Restore a valid OSPF configuration to /etc/frr/ospfd.conf on
mswitch0 (router-id 172.16.30.254, network statements for 172.16.10.0/24,
172.16.20.0/24, 172.16.30.0/24, and 10.2.2.0/24 in area 0), then run
`systemctl restart frr` on mswitch0 to bring ospfd back up.
=== END ===
```